<p class="para" id="av_1.0-ch01_s09_p01"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)">This section describes some of the fundamental mathematical skills you will need to complete the questions and problems in this text. For some of you, this discussion will serve as a review, whereas others may be encountering at least some of the ideas and techniques for the first time. We will introduce other mathematical skills in subsequent Essential Skills sections as the need arises. Be sure you are familiar with the topics discussed here before you start the <a class="xref" title="http://chemwiki.ucdavis.edu/Wikitexts/Howard_University/General_Chemistry%3A_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_1%3A__Atomic_Structure/Chapter_1%3A_Introduction" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Howard_University/General_Chemistry%3A_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_1%3A__Atomic_Structure/Chapter_1%3A_Introduction" rel="internal">Chapter 1 &quot;Introduction to Chemistry&quot;</a> problems.</span></p>

<div class="section" id="av_1.0-ch01_s09_s01">
<span id="Measurement"></span><div mt-section-origin="Courses/Howard_University/General_Chemistry:_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_1:__Atomic_Structure/Chapter_1:_Introduction/Chapter_1.8:_Essential_Skills_I" class="mt-section"><span id="Measurement"></span><h2 id="Measurement-17283"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)">Measurement</span></h2>

<div class="section" id="av_1.0-ch01_s09_s01_s01">
<span id="Instruments_too_Measure_With"></span><div mt-section-origin="Courses/Howard_University/General_Chemistry:_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_1:__Atomic_Structure/Chapter_1:_Introduction/Chapter_1.8:_Essential_Skills_I" class="mt-section"><span id="Instruments_too_Measure_With"></span><h2 id="Instruments_too_Measure_With-17283"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)">Instruments too Measure With</span></h2>

<div class="informalfigure large medium-height block">
<p style="text-align: center;"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)"><a class="thumb" title="Image of a buret and graduated cylinder with the stopcock on the buret labeled." rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/27694/f9beef56465a95446fcd37c3ce3e30c0.jpg?revision=1"><img alt="Image of a buret and graduated cylinder with the stopcock on the buret labeled." class="internal default" style="width: 200px; height: 269px;" loading="lazy" width="200px" height="269px" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/27694/f9beef56465a95446fcd37c3ce3e30c0.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=200&amp;height=269" /></a></span></p>

<p class="para" style="text-align: center;"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)"><em><strong class="emphasis bold">Graduated glassware</strong> is used to deliver variable volumes of liquid.</em></span></p>
</div>

<div class="informalfigure large medium-height block">
<p style="text-align: center;"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)"><a class="thumb" title="Image of a10mL pipette and 100mL volumetric flask with the calibration marks labeled on both." rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/27695/861a693e86b67d5cf7c1b8bbeb10d225.jpg?revision=1"><img alt="Image of a10mL pipette and 100mL volumetric flask with the calibration marks labeled on both." class="internal default" style="width: 199px; height: 279px;" loading="lazy" width="199px" height="279px" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/27695/861a693e86b67d5cf7c1b8bbeb10d225.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=199&amp;height=279" /></a></span></p>

<p class="para"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)"><strong class="emphasis bold">Volumetric glassware</strong> is used to deliver (pipette) or contain (volumetric flask) a single volume accurately when filled to the calibration mark.</span></p>
</div>

<div class="informalfigure large medium-height block">
<p style="text-align: center;"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)"><a class="thumb" title="Image of a balance with weigh paper and a white powder on it measuring 16.43 grams." rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28740/db8cb035d869d6a9ab18c2eac465d65a.jpg?revision=1"><img alt="Image of a balance with weigh paper and a white powder on it measuring 16.43 grams." class="internal default" style="width: 160px; height: 119px;" loading="lazy" width="160px" height="119px" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28740/db8cb035d869d6a9ab18c2eac465d65a.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=160&amp;height=119" /></a></span></p>

<p class="para"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)">A <strong class="emphasis bold">balance</strong> is used to measure mass.</span></p>
</div>

<p class="para" id="av_1.0-ch01_s09_s01_s01_p01"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)">A variety of instruments are available for making direct measurements of the macroscopic properties of a chemical substance. For example, we usually measure the <span class="margin_term">volume<span class="glossdef">The</span><span class="glossdef"> amount of space occupied by a sample of matter.</span></span> of a liquid sample with pipettes, burets, graduated cylinders, and volumetric flasks, whereas we usually measure the <em class="emphasis">mass</em> of a solid or liquid substance with a balance. Measurements on an atomic or molecular scale, in contrast, require specialized instrumentation, such as the mass spectrometer described in <a class="xref" title="https://chem.libretexts.org/LibreTexts/Howard_University/General_Chemistry%3A_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_1%3A__Atomic_Structure/Chapter_1%3A_Introduction/Chapter_1.6%3A_Isotopes_and_Atomic_Masses" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Howard_University/General_Chemistry%3A_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_1%3A__Atomic_Structure/Chapter_1%3A_Introduction/Chapter_1.6%3A_Isotopes_and_Atomic_Masses" rel="internal">Section 1.6 &quot;Isotopes and Atomic Masses&quot;</a><a title="http://chemwiki.ucdavis.edu/Wikitexts/Howard_University/General_Chemistry%3A_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_1%3A__Atomic_Structure/Chapter_1%3A_Introduction/Chapter_1.6%3A_Isotopes_and_Atomic_Masses" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Howard_University/General_Chemistry%3A_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_1%3A__Atomic_Structure/Chapter_1%3A_Introduction/Chapter_1.6%3A_Isotopes_and_Atomic_Masses" rel="internal">.</a></span></p>
</div></div>

<div class="section" id="av_1.0-ch01_s09_s01_s02">
<span id="SI_Units"></span><div mt-section-origin="Courses/Howard_University/General_Chemistry:_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_1:__Atomic_Structure/Chapter_1:_Introduction/Chapter_1.8:_Essential_Skills_I" class="mt-section"><span id="SI_Units"></span><h2 id="SI_Units-17283"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)">SI Units</span></h2>

<p class="para" id="av_1.0-ch01_s09_s01_s02_p01"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)">All reported measurements must include an appropriate unit of measurement because to say that a substance has &ldquo;a mass of 10,&rdquo; for example, does not tell whether the mass was measured in grams, pounds, tons, or some other unit. To establish worldwide standards for the consistent measurement of important physical and chemical properties, an international body called the General Conference on Weights and Measures devised the <span class="margin_term">Syst&egrave;me internationale d&rsquo;unit&eacute;s (or SI)<span class="glossdef">A system of units based on metric units that requires measurements to be expressed in decimal form. There are seven base units in the SI system.</span></span>. The International System of Units is based on metric units and requires that measurements be expressed in decimal form. <a href="#av_1.0-ch01_s09_s01_s02_t01" rel="internal" class="xref">Table 1.8.1 </a> lists the seven base units of the SI system; all other SI units of measurement are derived from them.</span></p>

<p class="para" id="av_1.0-ch01_s09_s01_s02_p02"><span class="mt-font-size-(Default)">By attaching prefixes to the base unit, the magnitude of the unit is indicated; each prefix indicates that the base unit is multiplied by a specified power of 10. The prefixes, their symbols, and their numerical significance are given in <a href="#av_1.0-ch01_s09_s01_s02_t02" rel="internal" class="xref">Table 1.8.2 </a>. To study chemistry, you need to know the information presented in <a href="#av_1.0-ch01_s09_s01_s02_t01" rel="internal" class="xref">Table 1.8,1 &quot;SI Base Units&quot;</a> and <a href="#av_1.0-ch01_s09_s01_s02_t02" rel="internal" class="xref">Table 1.8.2 </a>.</span></p>

